{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"bafvm02oa","tool_use_id":"toolu_01P2rDNCFxrWbqQzzeK7ZBeD","status":"completed","output_file":"/tmp/claude-441489/-scratch-users-kushinm-auto-psych-outer-loop-live-runs-run2-repo/d98c862c-aafe-4ca5-8a2f-dcc7e763cffb/tasks/bafvm02oa.output","summary":"Background command \"D=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run2/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_0_repair_1\ncat << 'EOF' > $D/hypothesis.md\nPeople read a sequence flip by flip while keeping a running tally of how far heads lead tails, and they expect a random coin's lead to keep changing hands: the tally should leave even and come back, again and again. A sequence in which one face stays ahead for a long unbroken stretch — the tally not getting back to even for many flips, even if the lead stays small (HHTHTHTH is one or two heads ahead throughout) — looks like a coin stuck in favour of one face and so less random, by a shared penalty on the longest stretch the lead is held without a return to even (as a share of the length). The rest of the impression is the current models' (tally span against a personal expected span, switching judged by closeness to an ideal rate with a personal, positive weight, chunk variety and a rule-built penalty scaled by a personal pattern sensitivity, the final-streak penalty, a personal left/right lean); the claim is that how long a lead is held, not only how far the tally swings, counts against randomness.\nEOF\necho held_lead_duration_tally > $D/model_name.txt\npython3 - << 'PY'\np=\"/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run2/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_0_repair_1/candidate.py\"\ns=open(p).read()\nold=s[s.index(\"    # Switching judged by closeness\"):s.index(\"    # Shared reward on the share\")]\nnew='''    # Switching judged by closeness to a shared ideal rate, with a personal\n    # positive weight (log-normal, non-centred: no sign ambiguity between the\n    # weight and the ideal, which made the earlier fit multimodal).\n    mu_sw = pm.Normal(\"mu_sw\", mu=0.4, sigma=0.7)\n    sw_ideal = pm.math.sigmoid(mu_sw)\n    mu_log_kappa = pm.Normal(\"mu_log_kappa\", mu=0.0, sigma=1.0)\n    sigma_log_kappa = pm.HalfNormal(\"sigma_log_kappa\", sigma=0.5)\n    z_kappa = pm.Normal(\"z_kappa\", mu=0.0, sigma=1.0, shape=N_SLOTS)\n    kappa = pm.math.exp(mu_log_kappa + sigma_log_kappa * z_kappa)\n\n'''\ns=s.replace(old,new)\ns=s.replace(\"sw_p = sw_ideal[participant_id]\\n\",\"sw_p = sw_ideal\\n\")\ns=s.replace('omega = pm.Normal(\"omega\", mu=0.0, sigma=2.0)','omega = pm.Normal(\"omega\", mu=0.0, sigma=1.0)')\ns=s.replace(\"\"\"rest of the impression is the current best model's (tally span against a\npersonal expected span, switching against a personal ideal rate, chunk variety\"\"\",\"\"\"rest of the impression is the current models' (tally span against a personal\nexpected span, switching against a shared ideal rate with a personal positive\nweight, chunk variety\"\"\")\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s)\nPY\nsed -n 1,15p $D/candidate.py; grep -n \"kappa\\|sw_p\\|omega\" $D/candidate.py\n/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate --candidate-dir $D --responses $D/../../responses.csv 2>&1 | tail -5\" completed (exit code 0)","uuid":"83392c39-fcf4-47a9-b086-448178979f9b","session_id":"d98c862c-aafe-4ca5-8a2f-dcc7e763cffb"}
